<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nepalese Civil War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1996">1996</a>–<a href="/years/2006">2006</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Maoist insurgency in Nepal, People's War (Nepal), People's War in Nepal · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nepalese Civil War was an armed conflict from 1996 to 2006 between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-nepal-maoist-centre">Communist Party of Nepal (Maoist)</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pushpa-kamal-dahal">Pushpa Kamal Dahal</a> (Prachanda), and the Nepali monarchy and government. Some 17,000 people were killed. It ended with the Comprehensive Peace Agreement of November 2006, after the People's Movement had forced King Gyanendra to give up direct rule, and led to the abolition of the monarchy in 2008.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nepalese-civil-war.jpg" alt="Nepalese Civil War" width="320" height="256" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hoben7599 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nepalese_Civil_War_collage.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In January 1990 the United Left Front was formed and, together with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepali-congress">Nepali Congress</a>, became the backbone of the movement for democratic change. Communist groups uneasy about that alliance formed a parallel front, the United National People's Movement, which called for elections to a constituent assembly.</p><p>In November 1990 the Communist Party of Nepal (Unity Centre) was formed, and in January 1991 it set up the United People's Front of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> under Baburam Bhattarai to contest elections. In 1991 the front became the third-largest party in parliament, but it split over tactics: one group under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pushpa-kamal-dahal">Pushpa Kamal Dahal</a>, known as Prachanda, argued for immediate armed revolution, while Nirmal Lama's group held that Nepal was not yet ripe for it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 13 February 1996 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-nepal-maoist-centre">Communist Party of Nepal (Maoist)</a> began what it called the people's war, with the stated aim of overthrowing the monarchy and founding a people's republic, making wide use of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare. In June 2001 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birendra-of-nepal">King Birendra</a> and most of the royal family were killed in the royal massacre, and Gyanendra was crowned king. Peace talks begun in August 2001 collapsed on 23 November, when the Maoists attacked police and army posts in 42 districts. On 26 November the government declared a state of emergency and deployed the Nepal Army.</p><p>On 4 October 2002 King Gyanendra dismissed Prime Minister Sher Bahadur Deuba and took executive power. A ceasefire from 29 January 2003 ended on 27 August 2003, when the Maoists withdrew from it. On 1 February 2005 the king dissolved the government and banned news reports; the army arrested political leaders, journalists and activists, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telephone">telephone</a> and internet connections were cut. In November 2005 the Maoists agreed to work with opposition parties against the king.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>After weeks of pro-democracy protests in April 2006 the Maoists announced a truce, and the Comprehensive Peace Accord was signed on 21 November 2006 by Prime Minister Girija Prasad Koirala and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pushpa-kamal-dahal">Prachanda</a>. It let the Maoists take part in government and placed their weapons under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> monitoring.</p><p>More than 17,000 people were killed, over 4,000 by Maoists and over 8,200 by government forces between 1996 and 2005, and an estimated 100,000 to 150,000 were displaced. Parliament set up a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truth-and-reconciliation-commission-peru">Truth and Reconciliation Commission</a> and a commission on disappearances, both established in 2013. The Nepalese Army took control of the Maoist People's Liberation Army on 10 April 2012.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nepalese Civil War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the 2006 People's Movement ended King Gyanendra's direct rule and the <b>Maoist insurgency</b>, a peace agreement brought the Maoists into politics.</blockquote><a href="/state/federal-democratic-republic-of-nepal">Federal Democratic Republic of Nepal</a></li></ul></section>
